> Directed nearest matching is not a permutation

For source points \(p_i\), transformed working points \(x_i=Rp_i+t\), and
nonempty target \(q_j\), the directed objective is

$$
E(R,t)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N\min_{1\le j\le M}\|Rp_i+t-q_j\|^2.
$$
\alttext(Mean squared directed nearest-target distance sums one minimum target distance per transformed source.)

Matches are independent: several source points can select the same target, and
target points need not be used. This is neither a one-to-one assignment nor a
symmetric cloud distance. Source and target counts can differ. There is no
trimming, robust loss, rejection threshold, spatial index or initial-pose
option. To supply a coarse initial pose, explicitly transform the source first;
the returned pose then maps that supplied cloud, not the earlier original frame.
Strict-less ascending target scans resolve computed distance ties by first
index.

> Derive the planar rigid update

Freeze the actual nearest matches \(y_i=q_{j_i}\) for current working points
\(x_i\). Center them at \(\bar x,\bar y\) and write \(a_i=x_i-\bar x\),
\(b_i=y_i-\bar y\). For a chosen angle, differentiating the squared loss in
translation gives \(d=\bar y-R(\theta)\bar x\). The remaining loss is a constant
minus twice the centered alignment score:

$$
\sum_i\|R(\theta)a_i-b_i\|^2
=C-2(A\cos\theta+B\sin\theta),\qquad
A=\sum_i a_i\mathbin{\cdot}b_i,\quad
B=\sum_i(a_{ix}b_{iy}-a_{iy}b_{ix}).
$$
\alttext(The centered loss is a constant minus twice A cosine theta plus B sine theta; A is the dot sum and B the signed cross sum.)

Thus \(\theta=\operatorname{atan2}(B,A)\) maximizes the score when
\((A,B)\ne(0,0)\). At zero covariance every angle has the same frozen-match
loss; the implementation retains the computed atan2 choice. The update is
\(x_i\leftarrow R(\theta)x_i+d\), with accumulated pose
\(R_{new}=R(\theta)R_{old}\), \(t_{new}=R(\theta)t_{old}+d\).

In exact arithmetic, keeping the current matches gives an upper bound on the new
nearest-target objective, and the rigid minimization cannot increase the old
frozen-match loss:

$$
E_{nearest}(x_{k+1})\le E_{fixed,j_k}(x_{k+1})\le E_{nearest}(x_k).
$$
\alttext(After an update, newly searched nearest error is at most the error against frozen matches, which is at most the previous nearest error.)

This proves monotone convergence of the nonnegative objective values, not unique
correspondence, convergence to the true pose or a global optimum. Floating
rounding can cause tiny increases. The trace reports all three errors from
actual working points; its extra observations never drive the solve.

> Preserve the original known-pose example

The original 25 points form a slightly perturbed five-by-five grid. The target
is their transform by angle .12 radians and translation (.7,−.5). The original
report's program did not shuffle its target despite saying so; this program
preserves its actual ordering. The existing independent native tests do shuffle
the target for 30 additional known-pose cases. This generated geometric study
needs no unrelated dataset.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/icp_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto source = dm::geometry::icp_example_source();
    const auto target =
        dm::geometry::icp_transform_points(source, .12, {.7, -.5});
    const auto h = dm::geometry::icp_trace(source, target, 100, 1e-15);
    std::print("| step | before RMSE | after fixed RMSE | after nearest RMSE |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: " "|\n");
    std::vector<double> steps, before, fixed, nearest;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < h.steps.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &s = h.steps[i];
        std::print("| {} | {:.12g} | {:.12g} | {:.12g} |\n", i + 1, s.pre_rmse,
                   s.post_fixed_rmse, s.post_nearest_rmse);
        steps.push_back(i + 1);
        before.push_back(s.pre_rmse);
        fixed.push_back(s.post_fixed_rmse);
        nearest.push_back(s.post_nearest_rmse);
    }
    std::print("Applied angle .12, shift (.7, -.5); recovered angle {:.12g}, shift ({:.12g}, {:.12g}), " "final RMSE {:.12g}, updates {}.\n",
               h.run.result.angle, h.run.result.translation.x,
               h.run.result.translation.y, h.run.result.rmse,
               h.run.result.iterations);
    auto cloud = dm::geometry::icp_alignment_plot(source, target, h.run);
    cloud.title("Original 25-point alignment").y_limits(-2, 16);
    auto errors = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    errors.line(steps, before, "before", {100, 116, 139}, 2).line(steps, fixed,
                                                                  "fixed",
                                                                  {37, 99, 235},
                                                                  2).line(
        steps, nearest, "nearest", {220, 38, 38}, 2).title(
        "Actual errors around each update").x_label("completed update").y_label(
        "RMSE").y_limits(0, 2);
    auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
    layout.add(cloud).add(errors).size(1100, 500).title("The original pose and its actual error history").fit_panels().save_svg("build/icp_original.svg");
}
```
