> What the checks establish

Native sanitizer tests inspect every actual match, rigid update, accumulated
pose and all three errors over 500 generated registrations. They compare errors
with independent widened accumulation and check the two exact monotonic
inequalities with numerical tolerances. The original shuffled known-pose and
error-profile tests pass. One-point final-error/zero-budget controls, strict
plateau versus forced budgets, many-to-one ties, complete quotas, ownership,
invalid input and arithmetic overflow are tested separately. Frozen comparisons
preserve 2000 legacy poses and update counts within 1e-13, while intentionally
correcting the returned error's timing. An independent Fraction/70-digit Decimal
oracle verifies 259 cases, 1295 actual steps and 7450 selected matches,
comparing fixed-match losses with their closed-form optimum and checking
exact-input centroids, fresh nearest errors and rigid-distance preservation.
Fresh coverage checks pass all 53 definitions, 14 compiled public examples and
four clangd hovers.

The method has demonstrated accurate local refinement on the original example,
not universal pose recovery. Finite precision, degeneracy, symmetries, initial
pose, asymmetric sampling and outliers can all matter. A nonincreasing objective
alone cannot justify the old claim that the true pose is recovered from any
reasonable-looking overlap. Applying an accumulated pose once can also differ by
rounding from the actual repeatedly updated points retained by `icp_run`.

The primary source is [P. J. Besl and N. D. McKay (1992), A Method for
Registration of 3-D Shapes](https://doi.org/10.1109/34.121791), IEEE
Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence 14(2), 239–256; an
[original paper
copy](https://graphics.stanford.edu/courses/cs348a-21-winter/Handouts/Besl92.pdf)
is available. This report derives a planar point-to-point specialization with
brute-force discrete nearest matches, rather than the paper's quaternion-based
3-D fit or its other geometric representations. It preserves the
implementation's pre-update RMSE-change stop, not a universal stopping or
pose-success theorem. The original paper's least-squares/ICP formulation and
monotonicity proof on pages 243–244 were checked; this implementation updates
the evolving cloud and composes increments, whereas the paper expresses each
registration against the original source. These agree in exact arithmetic but
can round differently.
